<main id="top"><div class="wrap"><div class="hero">
  <div>
    <span class="eb"><i></i> Series IV — now striking</span>
    <h1><span class="shine">Every wasted token</span><br><em>deserves</em> a medal.</h1>
    <p class="lede">We take the counters, credits and contexts you burned through and press them into <strong>44&nbsp;mm of solid, hand-finished alloy</strong>. Die-struck five times, edge-reeded, serialised on the rim, delivered in a velvet-lined case. Not a JPEG. A coin you can drop on a bar and hear.</p>
    <div class="cta"><a class="btn gold" href="#collection">View the collection</a><a class="btn ghost" href="#about">How we strike</a></div>
    <div class="hs"><div><span>44 mm</span><small>Diameter</small></div><div><span>3.8 mm</span><small>Thickness</small></div><div><span>132</span><small>Edge reeds</small></div><div><span>5×</span><small>Strikes per face</small></div></div>
  </div>
  <figure class="stage"><canvas id="coin-hero"></canvas><figcaption>Reference specimen — Series IV proof · 10,000 Tokens</figcaption></figure>
</div></div>

<div class="tick"><div class="tin" id="tick"></div></div>

<section id="about" class="wrap rv">
  <div class="sh"><div><div class="sn">01 — The Mint</div><h2>A small foundry with an<br>unreasonable standard.</h2></div>
  <p>Two presses, one lathe, four people, and a stubborn refusal to ship a coin that doesn't ring true when you flick it.</p></div>
  <div class="g3">
    <div class="card"><div class="gl">✦</div><h3>Struck, never cast</h3><p>Casting is fast and soft. We cut hardened tool-steel dies and strike each blank five times at 180 tonnes, so the relief comes up crisp enough to read a serif two millimetres tall.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="gl">◈</div><h3>Finished by hand</h3><p>Every coin is tumbled, then hand-relieved: fields brought to a mirror while the raised emblem keeps its frosted cameo. It's why the highlight travels across the face instead of sitting on it.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="gl">⬡</div><h3>Numbered for life</h3><p>The rim carries an incuse serial and the edition mark. We keep the register; if a coin changes hands, the ledger follows it. Dies are defaced when an edition closes.</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="ab">
    <div>
      <p class="drop">WastedToken Mint began as a joke on a whiteboard — a tally of everything an engineering floor had burned through in a quarter, drawn as a coin. Somebody cut the die anyway. The first hundred pieces went out in padded envelopes and came back as photographs from twenty-one countries: pinned to monitors, carried in pockets, slid across tables at the end of long arguments.</p>
      <p>Ten seasons later the joke has a foundry. We still strike in small, closed editions — never reopened, never restruck, never "remastered". When an edition sells out the dies are scored across the face and retired to the wall. What you hold is the whole run.</p>
      <p>Blanks are chosen for weight and voice, not price: a coin should land with authority and hold its edge for a lifetime of nervous thumbing. Everything leaves the bench with a capsule, a case, a certificate on cotton stock, and a rim serial tied to a line in the register.</p>
    </div>
    <dl class="spec">
      <div><dt>Diameter</dt><dd>44.00 mm</dd></div><div><dt>Thickness</dt><dd>3.80 mm</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Mass</dt><dd>42 – 48 g by alloy</dd></div><div><dt>Edge</dt><dd>132 reeds, incuse serial</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Relief</dt><dd>Dual, frosted cameo</dd></div><div><dt>Tolerance</dt><dd>± 0.04 mm</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Packaging</dt><dd>Capsule + velvet case</dd></div><div><dt>Register</dt><dd>Permanent, transferable</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="collection" class="wrap rv">
  <div class="sh"><div><div class="sn">02 — The Collection</div><h2>Four editions.<br>Two still open.</h2></div>
  <p>Each edition is a different alloy, denomination and die. Once a run closes, it closes for good.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="edition" data-edition="bronze" data-state="available">
      <div class="cw"><canvas class="edition-canvas"></canvas></div>
      <div class="em"><div class="et"><span class="en">Bronze</span><span class="ed">1,000 Tokens</span></div>
        <span class="badge ok">● Available — 612 of 1,500</span>
        <p class="de">The everyday piece. Warm cast-bronze alloy, hand-antiqued field, satin cameo. It will darken to your pocket.</p>
        <div class="pr">$48</div><button class="buy">Add to cart</button></div>
    </div>
    <div class="edition" data-edition="silver" data-state="available">
      <div class="cw"><canvas class="edition-canvas"></canvas></div>
      <div class="em"><div class="et"><span class="en">Silver</span><span class="ed">5,000 Tokens</span></div>
        <span class="badge ok">● Available — 188 of 750</span>
        <p class="de">Fine silver over a nickel core, mirror fields against a frosted emblem. The proof finish that photographs like a coin should.</p>
        <div class="pr">$96</div><button class="buy">Add to cart</button></div>
    </div>
    <div class="edition" data-edition="gold" data-state="sold-out">
      <div class="cw"><canvas class="edition-canvas"></canvas></div>
      <div class="em"><div class="et"><span class="en">Gold</span><span class="ed">10,000 Tokens</span></div>
        <span class="badge out">■ SOLD OUT — 250 of 250 struck</span>
        <p class="de">SOLD OUT. The flagship: 24k gilt over brass, double-frosted cameo, serialised 001–250. Dies defaced 12 March. No restrikes, ever.</p>
        <div class="pr">$210 — edition closed</div><div class="lock">Sold out</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="edition" data-edition="black" data-state="coming-soon">
      <div class="cw"><canvas class="edition-canvas"></canvas></div>
      <div class="em"><div class="et"><span class="en">Obsidian</span><span class="ed">25,000 Tokens</span></div>
        <span class="badge soon">◆ Minting next season — Winter 2026</span>
        <p class="de">Black ruthenium over sterling with an oil-slick sheen in the relief. Dies are being cut now; the Winter release opens to register holders first.</p>
        <div class="pr">Winter 2026</div><div class="lock">Not yet minted</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="process" class="wrap rv">
  <div class="sh"><div><div class="sn">03 — The Process</div><h2>Whiteboard to pocket<br>in eleven weeks.</h2></div>
  <p>Nothing is outsourced past the blanking press. We cut, strike, finish, serialise and pack under one roof.</p></div>
  <div class="steps">
    <div class="step"><b>I</b><h4>Engrave</h4><p>The emblem is modelled in relief, cut into hardened tool steel on a four-axis engraver, then hand-sharpened at the letter edges.</p></div>
    <div class="step"><b>II</b><h4>Strike</h4><p>Annealed blanks take five hits at 180 tonnes, with a re-anneal between the third and fourth so the metal flows rather than tears.</p></div>
    <div class="step"><b>III</b><h4>Finish</h4><p>Fields polished to a mirror; the raised relief masked and bead-blasted to a frost. Then plating, if the edition calls for it.</p></div>
    <div class="step"><b>IV</b><h4>Register</h4><p>The rim is incused with its serial, the coin capsuled, and its number written into the permanent register in your name.</p></div>
  </div>
</section></main>

